  <p style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 40px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 60px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <div style="border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-top: 0;">Records</p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You must keep records to verify your deductions. You should keep receipts, canceled checks, financial account statements, and other documentation to support your deductions. See Publication 552, Recordkeeping for Individuals.</p>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
        <p style="font-weight: bold; margin-top: 0;">Comments and suggestions.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">We welcome your comments about this publication and your suggestions for improvements. You can e-mail us while visiting our web site at <span data-field="website_url">{{ website_url }}</span>.</p>
        <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">You can write to us at the following addresses:</p>
        <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4; padding-left: 20px;">
          <li>Internal Revenue Service<br>
            Technical Publications Branch<br>
            W.C.AR-MP FP<br>
            111 Constitution Ave.<br>
            Washington, DC 20224</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div style="border: 1px solid #ccc; padding: 10px;">
        <p style="font-size: 0.9em;">We respond to many letters by telephone. Therefore, it would help if you would include your phone name, number, including the area code, in your correspondence.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Generally, you apply the 2% limit after you apply any other deduction limit. For example, you apply the 50% (or 60%) limit on business-related meals and entertainment (discussed later under Travel, Transportation, Meal, Entertainment, and Gift Expenses) before you apply the 2% limit.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Deductions subject to the 2% limit are discussed in the following sections. You may also see them in categories within in which you report them on Schedule A: Miscellaneous Itemized Deductions, Medical Expenses, and Other Expenses.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Impairment-related work expenses. If you have a physical or mental disability, certain expenses you incur that allow you to work may not be subject to the 2% limit. See Impairment-Related Work Expenses under Deductions Not Subject to the 2% Limit.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Performing artists. If you are a qualified performing artist, you may deduct certain expenses incurred in the performance of your artistic services, even if they are not subject to the 2% limit. See Performing Artists under Deductions Not Subject to the 2% Limit.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">State and local government officials paid on a fee basis. If you performed services as an employee of a state or local government and you were paid in whole or in part on a fee basis, you can claim your travel, transportation, meal, entertainment, and gift expenses as an adjustment to gross income, rather than as a miscellaneous deduction. See Officials Paid on a Fee Basis under Deductions Not Subject to the 2% Limit.</h3>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
