  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Deductions Subject to the 2% Limit</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct certain expenses as miscellaneous itemized deductions on Schedule A (Form 1040). You can claim the amount of these expenses, but you must reduce the total of most miscellaneous itemized deductions by 2% of your adjusted gross income. If your adjusted gross income is the amount on Form 1040, line <span data-field="line_number">{{ line_number }}</span>.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <span data-field="date_stamp">{{ date_stamp }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 40px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 60px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Home Office</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">If you use a part of your home regularly and exclusively for business purposes, you may be able to deduct a part of the expenses of your home. To qualify, the area you use must be used exclusively and regularly for business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">1) As your principal place of business for any trade or business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">2) As a place to meet with your clients, patients, or customers in the normal course of your trade or business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">3) In the case of a separate structure not attached to your home, the structure must be used exclusively and regularly for business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">The regular and exclusive business use must be for the convenience of your employer and not appropriate for your personal use.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Principal place of business. If you have more than one place of business, the place where you spend the most time is your principal place of business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">1) You use it regularly and exclusively for administrative or management activities of your trade or business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">2) You have no other fixed location where you conduct your trade or business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Otherwise, the location of your principal place of business generally depends on the relative importance of the activities performed at each location and the time spent at each location.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Should records show that will give the information needed to deduct the home office expenses, you must keep receipts, canceled checks, or other documentation to support the expenses paid to prove the deductions you claim.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">More information. Get Publication 587 for more detailed information and a worksheet for figuring the deduction.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Damages for Breach of Employment Contract</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">If you break an employment contract, you can deduct damages you pay your former employer if the damages are attributable to the pay you received from that employer.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Depreciation on Computers or Cellular Telephones</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can claim a depreciation deduction for a computer or cellular telephone that you use in your work as an employee if you use it for business.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">1) For the convenience of your employer, and</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">2) Required as a condition of your employment.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">For the convenience of your employer. This means that you use the computer or cellular telephone for a substantial business reason of your employer. You must consider all facts in making this determination. Use of your computer or cellular telephone is generally for the convenience of your employer and is not required as a condition of your employment, you cannot claim a depreciation deduction for it.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Which depreciation method to use. You generally must depreciate your computer or cellular telephone using the Modified Accelerated Cost Recovery System (MACRS) as described in Publication 946. You cannot take a section 179 deduction for the item or claim an accelerated depreciation deduction for the item if you use it for both personal and business purposes unless you meet the more-than-50% test. (But if you use it for more than 50% for business, you can claim a section 179 deduction and depreciation deductions using ADS and GDS are explained in Publication 946).</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top: 50px;">
    <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Business Liability Insurance</h3>
    <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct insurance premiums you paid for protection against personal liability for wrongful acts on the job.</p>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
      <span data-field="page_number">{{ page_number }}</span>
    </div>
    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <span data-field="date_stamp">{{ date_stamp }}</span>
    </div>
  </div>
  <p style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 40px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 60px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Your use of a computer or cellular telephone in connection with investments (described later under Other Expenses) does not count as use in your work. However, you may be able to deduct the expenses of your computer or cellular telephone if you use it for business.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">For more information, see Premium Use Test in chapter 4 of Publication 946.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Exception for computer used in a home office. The more-than-50% use test does not apply to a computer used in a home office. You can deduct the expenses of the computer if you use it regularly and exclusively for business. You must keep records to show that you use it exclusively and regularly for business. If you use it for both business and personal purposes, you must use the more-than-50% test. For more information, see Publication 946.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Deductions for computers and other items used in a home office, see Business Furniture and Equipment in Publication 535.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Report your depreciation deduction. Use Form 4562 to claim the depreciation deduction for a computer or cellular telephone. You must keep records to show that you use it exclusively and regularly for business. Complete Part I of Form 4562 if you are claiming a section 179 deduction.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Do not use Form 4562 to claim the depreciation deduction for a computer or cellular telephone if you use it for both business and personal purposes. In that case, you must use the more-than-50% test. For more information, see Publication 946.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You must keep records to prove your deductions. Keep receipts, canceled checks, or other documentation to support the expenses paid to prove the deductions you claim.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Dues to Chambers of Commerce and Professional Societies</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You may be able to deduct dues paid to professional organizations (such as bar associations and medical associations) and to chambers of commerce and similar organizations if membership helps you carry out the duties of your job. Similar organizations include:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>Boards of trade,</li>
        <li>Business leagues,</li>
        <li>Civic or public service organizations,</li>
        <li>Real estate boards, and</li>
        <li>Trade associations.</li>
      </ul>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Appraisal Fees</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct appraisal fees if you pay them to a professional to determine the fair market value of donated property.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Certain Casualty and Theft Losses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct casualty or theft losses as a miscellaneous itemized deduction subject to the 2% limit if the loss is not covered by insurance. First report the loss in Section B of Form 8864, and then claim the deduction on Schedule A (Form 1040). For more information, see Publication 547, Casualties and Theft Losses.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Clerical Help and Office Rent</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct office expenses, such as rent and clerical help, if you use the office for your trade or business and the expenses are directly related to the activity of your trade or business.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Depreciation on Computer or Cellular Telephones</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct a depreciation deduction for a computer or cellular telephone that you use in your work as an employee if you use it for business. See the section on Depreciation on Computers or Cellular Telephones for more information.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Excess Deductions of an Estate</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">If an estate's total deductions in its last tax year are more than its gross income, the beneficiaries can deduct the excess. Do not include deductions for personal exemption and charitable contributions. For more information, see Publication 559, Survivors, Executors, and Administrators.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <div style="margin-bottom: 30px;">
