  <p style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 40px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 60px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Research Expenses of a College Professor</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">If you are a college professor, you can deduct your research expenses, including travel, supplies, and other expenses, if they are directly related to the field of your teaching duties. You must have the expenses be directly related to the field of your teaching duties. You must keep records to prove these expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Generally, you can deduct amounts you spend for tools used in your work if the tools were bought for your work. You must keep records to prove these expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Travel, Transportation, Meal, Entertainment, and Gift Expenses. If you are an employee and have ordinary and necessary business-related expenses for travel away from home, you may be able to deduct these expenses. You must keep records to prove these expenses. Generally, you must file Form 2106 or 2106-EZ to claim these expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Travel expenses. Travel expenses are those incurred while traveling away from home for your employer. You can deduct travel expenses if they are directly related to your job. You must keep records to prove these expenses. Travel expenses may include:</p>
      <ul style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4; padding-left: 20px;">
        <li>The cost of getting to and from your business destination (air, rail, bus, etc.).</li>
        <li>Meals and lodging while away from home.</li>
        <li>Taxi fares.</li>
        <li>Baggage charges, and</li>
        <li>Cleaning and laundry expenses.</li>
      </ul>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Travel expenses are discussed more fully in chapter 1 of Publication 463.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Temporary work assignment. If your assignment or work is temporary, you may be able to deduct the expenses incurred while you are away from home in a single location is realistically eligible for deduction. You must keep records to prove these expenses. If the assignment is temporary, unless there are facts and circumstances that indicate it is not.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Indefinite work assignment. If your assignment or work is indefinite, you may be able to deduct the expenses incurred while you are away from home in a single location is realistically eligible for deduction. You must keep records to prove these expenses. If the assignment is indefinite, unless there are facts and circumstances that indicate it is not.</h3>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Federal crime investigation and prosecution. If you are a federal employee participating in a federal crime investigation or prosecution, you are not subject to the 2% limit. You can deduct the expenses incurred while you are traveling.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Generally, the Attorney General must certify that you are traveling.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Local transportation expenses. Local transportation expenses are the expenses of getting from one workplace to another within the same city or town. You can deduct these expenses if they are directly related to the activity of your trade or business. You must keep records to prove these expenses. The standard mileage rate for 2001 is <span data-field="mileage_rate">{{ mileage_rate }}</span> cents per mile. See Publication 463 for more information on travel and car expenses.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Work at two places in a day. If you work at two places in a day, you may be able to deduct the travel expenses between those two places. You must keep records to prove these expenses. The standard mileage rate for 2001 is <span data-field="mileage_rate">{{ mileage_rate }}</span> cents per mile. See Publication 463 for more information on travel and car expenses.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Temporary workplace. You can deduct expenses incurred in getting between your home and a temporary workplace if the workplace is outside the metropolitan area where you live and normally work.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">For this purpose, a workplace is generally considered temporary if your work there is realistically expected to last for less than one year. If your work there is realistically expected to last for more than one year, it is generally considered a permanent workplace. For more information, see chapter 4 of Publication 463.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Home office. You can deduct expenses for a home office if your principal place of business is the home office. You must keep records to prove these expenses. If your home office is your principal place of business, you must use the more-than-50% test. For more information, see Publication 587.</h3>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

<div class="page">
  <div style="display: flex; justify-content: space-between; align-items: flex-start; margin-bottom: 20px;">
