  <p style="font-size: 0.9em; margin-bottom: 40px;">The type and rule above prints on all proofs including departmental reproduction proofs. MUST be removed before printing.</p>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 60px;">
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Meals and entertainment. Generally, you can deduct entertainment expenses (including entertainment-related meals) only if they are directly related to the active conduct of your trade or business. You must keep records to prove these expenses. You can deduct only 50% of your business-related meal expenses. You must keep records to prove these expenses. You can deduct only 50% of your business-related meal expenses. You must keep records to prove these expenses.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Gift expenses. Generally, you may deduct up to 2% of your adjusted gross income for gifts you give to any individual during the year.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">• Identical, widely distributed items costing $4 or less per item are not deductible.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">• Signs, racks, and promotional materials to be displayed on the business premises of the recipient.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Union Dues and Expenses</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct dues and initiation fees you pay for union membership. You can also deduct assessments for benefit payments made to a union if the union provides funds for the payment of sick, accident, or death benefits. You must keep records to prove these expenses. You must keep records to prove these expenses.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Work Clothes and Uniforms</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You can deduct the cost and upkeep of work clothes if the clothes are required as a condition of your employment.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">1) You must wear them as a condition of your employment.</p>
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">The clothes are not suitable for everyday wear.</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">It is not enough that you wear distinctive clothing. Examples of work clothes that are not suitable for everyday wear include: uniforms, protective gear, and clothing required by your employer. It is not enough that you wear distinctive clothing. Examples of work clothes that are not suitable for everyday wear include: uniforms, protective gear, and clothing required by your employer.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Examples of work clothes that are not suitable for everyday wear include: uniforms, protective gear, and clothing required by your employer.</p>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Protective clothing. You can deduct the cost of protective clothing if it is required as a condition of your employment. Examples of work clothes may be required to wear safety items; carpenters, cement workers, chemical workers, and others may be required to wear protective clothing.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Military uniforms. You generally cannot deduct the cost of military uniforms if you are on active duty in the military. If you are in the military reserve, you can deduct the cost of your uniform if it is required as a condition of your employment. You must keep records to prove these expenses.</h3>
      <h3 style="font-size: 18px; margin-bottom: 10px;">Tax Preparation Fees</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">You pay tax preparation fees in the year you pay them. Thus, on your 2001 return, you can deduct the fees you paid in 2001. You must keep records to prove these expenses. You must keep records to prove these expenses.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 0.9em; line-height: 1.4;">Deductions expenses of preparing tax schedules relating to the tax year for which you are filing the return.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

